{
  "root": "main_stack",
  "state": {},
  "elements": {
    "main_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "md"
      },
      "children": [
        "title_h2",
        "intro_text",
        "section_heading_h2",
        "prose_style_h3",
        "hemingway_prose_stack",
        "fitzgerald_prose_stack",
        "dialogue_h3",
        "hemingway_dialogue_stack",
        "fitzgerald_dialogue_stack",
        "thematic_h3",
        "hemingway_thematic_stack",
        "fitzgerald_thematic_stack",
        "explore_further_h2",
        "explore_buttons_stack",
        "sources_h2",
        "sources_stack"
      ]
    },
    "title_h2": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Literary Styles: Hemingway vs. Fitzgerald",
        "variant": "h2"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "intro_text": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Ernest Hemingway and F. Scott Fitzgerald, contemporaries and acquaintances of the \"Lost Generation,\" each forged distinctive literary styles that profoundly influenced 20th-century literature. While both writers captured the spirit and disillusionment of their era, their approaches to prose, dialogue, and thematic exploration diverged significantly, offering contrasting lenses through which to view the human condition."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "section_heading_h2": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Prose, Dialogue, and Thematic Differences",
        "variant": "h2"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "prose_style_h3": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Prose Style",
        "variant": "h3"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_prose_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "hemingway_prose_label",
        "hemingway_prose_desc",
        "hemingway_prose_example_label",
        "hemingway_prose_example_code"
      ]
    },
    "hemingway_prose_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Ernest Hemingway:",
        "variant": "label"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_prose_desc": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Known for his terse, minimalist, and direct prose. Hemingway employed short, declarative sentences, simple vocabulary, and a journalistic precision. His \"iceberg theory\" suggested that much of the story's meaning lies beneath the surface, implied rather than stated."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_prose_example_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Example (from A Farewell to Arms):",
        "variant": "caption"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_prose_example_code": {
      "type": "ConsoleLog",
      "props": {
        "code": "The world breaks every one and afterward many are strong at the broken places. But those that will not break it kills. It kills the very good and the very gentle and the very brave impartially. If you are none of these you can be sure it will kill you too but there will be no special hurry.",
        "language": "text"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_prose_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "fitzgerald_prose_label",
        "fitzgerald_prose_desc",
        "fitzgerald_prose_example_label",
        "fitzgerald_prose_example_code"
      ]
    },
    "fitzgerald_prose_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "F. Scott Fitzgerald:",
        "variant": "label"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_prose_desc": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Characterized by lyrical, ornate, and evocative prose. Fitzgerald's writing is rich with imagery, metaphor, and a more complex sentence structure. He used language to create a sense of beauty, nostalgia, and often a melancholic grandeur, reflecting the glamorous yet fragile world of the Jazz Age."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_prose_example_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Example (from The Great Gatsby):",
        "variant": "caption"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_prose_example_code": {
      "type": "ConsoleLog",
      "props": {
        "code": "Gatsby believed in the green light, the orgastic future that year by year recedes before us. It eluded us then, but that's no matter—tomorrow we will run faster, stretch out our arms farther.... And one fine morning—So we beat on, boats against the current, borne back ceaselessly into the past.",
        "language": "text"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "dialogue_h3": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Dialogue",
        "variant": "h3"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_dialogue_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "hemingway_dialogue_label",
        "hemingway_dialogue_desc",
        "hemingway_dialogue_example_label",
        "hemingway_dialogue_example_code"
      ]
    },
    "hemingway_dialogue_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Ernest Hemingway:",
        "variant": "label"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_dialogue_desc": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "His dialogue is realistic, clipped, and understated, mirroring natural speech patterns. Characters often use short, blunt sentences, with much of the emotional subtext conveyed through what is not said. This brevity enhances realism and often suggests a stoic endurance."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_dialogue_example_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Example (from The Sun Also Rises):",
        "variant": "caption"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_dialogue_example_code": {
      "type": "ConsoleLog",
      "props": {
        "code": "Isn't it pretty to think so?",
        "language": "text"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_dialogue_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "fitzgerald_dialogue_label",
        "fitzgerald_dialogue_desc",
        "fitzgerald_dialogue_example_label",
        "fitzgerald_dialogue_example_code"
      ]
    },
    "fitzgerald_dialogue_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "F. Scott Fitzgerald:",
        "variant": "label"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_dialogue_desc": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Fitzgerald's dialogue is typically witty, sophisticated, and often dramatic. His characters engage in more extended and articulate exchanges, which serve to reveal their social standing, aspirations, and the complex web of relationships. The dialogue often reflects the polished yet superficial conversations of the wealthy elite."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_dialogue_example_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Example (from The Great Gatsby):",
        "variant": "caption"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_dialogue_example_code": {
      "type": "ConsoleLog",
      "props": {
        "code": "Her voice is full of money,\" he said suddenly. That was it. I'd never understood before. It was full of money—that was the inexhaustible charm that rose and fell in it, the jingle of it, the cymbals' song of it….",
        "language": "text"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "thematic_h3": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Thematic Approaches",
        "variant": "h3"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_thematic_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "hemingway_thematic_label",
        "hemingway_thematic_desc",
        "hemingway_thematic_key_works_label",
        "hemingway_thematic_key_works_text"
      ]
    },
    "hemingway_thematic_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Ernest Hemingway:",
        "variant": "label"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_thematic_desc": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Explored themes of war, loss, disillusionment, masculinity, courage (\"grace under pressure\"), and the search for meaning in a world scarred by conflict. His characters often grapple with existential struggles, testing their limits against nature and fate."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_thematic_key_works_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Key Works:",
        "variant": "caption"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "hemingway_thematic_key_works_text": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "The Sun Also Rises (post-war disillusionment, lost generation), A Farewell to Arms (tragedy of war, love, loss), The Old Man and the Sea (struggle, endurance, dignity in defeat)."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_thematic_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "fitzgerald_thematic_label",
        "fitzgerald_thematic_desc",
        "fitzgerald_thematic_key_works_label",
        "fitzgerald_thematic_key_works_text"
      ]
    },
    "fitzgerald_thematic_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "F. Scott Fitzgerald:",
        "variant": "label"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_thematic_desc": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Focused on the American Dream, the corrupting influence of wealth, social class, love, nostalgia, and the inevitable decay of idealism. His works often captured the glamour, excess, and ultimate hollowness of the Jazz Age, highlighting the tension between aspiration and reality."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_thematic_key_works_label": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Key Works:",
        "variant": "caption"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_thematic_key_works_text": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "The Great Gatsby (the American Dream's allure and emptiness, social class, love), Tender Is the Night (decadence, psychological breakdown, love and ruin), This Side of Paradise (youthful idealism, disillusionment)."
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "explore_further_h2": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Explore Further",
        "variant": "h2"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "explore_buttons_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "hemingway_button",
        "fitzgerald_button"
      ]
    },
    "hemingway_button": {
      "type": "Button",
      "props": {
        "label": "Hemingway Society"
      },
      "on": {
        "press": {
          "action": "openUrl",
          "params": {
            "url": "https://www.hemingwaysociety.org/"
          }
        }
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_button": {
      "type": "Button",
      "props": {
        "label": "F. Scott Fitzgerald Society"
      },
      "on": {
        "press": {
          "action": "openUrl",
          "params": {
            "url": "https://www.fscottfitzgeraldsociety.org/"
          }
        }
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "sources_h2": {
      "type": "Text",
      "props": {
        "text": "Sources",
        "variant": "h2"
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "sources_stack": {
      "type": "Stack",
      "props": {
        "direction": "vertical",
        "gap": "sm"
      },
      "children": [
        "hemingway_source_button",
        "fitzgerald_source_button",
        "sparknotes_source_button"
      ]
    },
    "hemingway_source_button": {
      "type": "Button",
      "props": {
        "label": "Ernest Hemingway Society"
      },
      "on": {
        "press": {
          "action": "openUrl",
          "params": {
            "url": "https://www.hemingwaysociety.org/"
          }
        }
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "fitzgerald_source_button": {
      "type": "Button",
      "props": {
        "label": "F. Scott Fitzgerald Society"
      },
      "on": {
        "press": {
          "action": "openUrl",
          "params": {
            "url": "https://www.fscottfitzgeraldsociety.org/"
          }
        }
      },
      "children": []
    },
    "sparknotes_source_button": {
      "type": "Button",
      "props": {
        "label": "SparkNotes Literature Guides"
      },
      "on": {
        "press": {
          "action": "openUrl",
          "params": {
            "url": "https://www.sparknotes.com/"
          }
        }
      },
      "children": []
    }
  }
}
